<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eugène de Beauharnais</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1781">1781</a>–<a href="/years/1824">1824</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Prince Eugène</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eugène de Beauharnais was the son of Joséphine and the adopted stepson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>. As viceroy he governed the Napoleonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> between 1805 and 1814 and commanded armies in the campaigns of 1809 and 1812. In 1810 he was named heir to the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, and after Napoleon's fall his father-in-law, the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, made him Duke of Leuchtenberg in 1817.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eugene-de-beauharnais.jpg" alt="Eugène de Beauharnais" width="320" height="433" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andrea Appiani · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Prince_Eugene_de_Beauharnais,_Viceroy_of_Italy_and_Duke_of_Leuchtenberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eugène Rose de Beauharnais was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 3 September 1781, the son of Viscount Alexandre de Beauharnais and Marie-Josèphe Rose Tascher de la Pagerie, both born in Martinique, who separated when he was three. His father, a general in the early Revolutionary Wars, was guillotined on 23 July 1794 after losing the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a>. Eugène soon joined the Revolutionary Army as an orderly to General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lazare-hoche">Lazare Hoche</a>. He served as aide-de-camp to his stepfather in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, followed him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, where he was wounded at the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, and commanded the chasseurs of the Consular Guard.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> was crowned King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on 26 May 1805, after his brother Joseph and then Louis had refused the throne, and on 7 June he announced Eugène as Viceroy of Italy. After Austerlitz Napoleon arranged Eugène's marriage to Princess Augusta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, daughter of King Maximilian I Joseph, and adopted him on 12 January 1806. He was excluded from the French succession but declared heir presumptive to the Italian throne. As viceroy he built fortifications, roads and public works and introduced the Napoleonic codes.</p><p>In 1809 he commanded the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, lost the Battle of Sacile against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archduke-john-of-austria">Archduke John</a>, then won at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piave">Piave</a> and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raab</a> and fought at Wagram. In 1812 he led IV Corps in the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, fighting at Borodino and the Berezina, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-murat">Joachim Murat</a> left he took command of the remnants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grande-armee">Grande Armée</a>.</p><p>In 1814, despite Murat's defection and pressure from his father-in-law, he refused to change sides, and he fought a series of retreats up the Po Valley. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s abdication he signed the Convention of Schiarino-Rizzino on 16 April, and a rising in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> on 20 April ended his hope of being crowned. He gave up the kingdom at Mantua on 23 April.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Eugène retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> in June 1814 and then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on his mother's death, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I of Russia</a> received him honourably. He gave up politics and stayed neutral during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>. On 14 November 1817 he became Duke of Leuchtenberg and Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>. He lived in Munich managing estates and adding to his art collection. He died there on 21 February 1824, aged 42. Two of his daughters became queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>. Historians consider him one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s most able relatives.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eugène de Beauharnais” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>These were combined with Frankfurt, Aschaffenburg and Wetzlar to form the Grand Duchy of Frankfurt, with Napoleon's stepson <b>Eugène de Beauharnais</b> named as Dalberg's successor.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-frankfurt">Grand Duchy of Frankfurt</a></li><li><blockquote>On 26 May 1805 Napoleon crowned himself king of Italy in Milan Cathedral with the Iron Crown of Lombardy, and appointed his stepson <b>Eugène de Beauharnais</b> as viceroy.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-italy-napoleonic">Kingdom of Italy (Napoleonic)</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1817 the King of Bavaria created the title Duke of Leuchtenberg for <b>Eugène de Beauharnais</b>, whose descendants married into the Swedish, Portuguese and Russian royal families.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-leuchtenberg">Landgraviate of Leuchtenberg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1808" aria-label="Paris on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4604 458 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eugène de Beauharnais"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5054 728h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.86,2.35&amp;year=1808">What was here in 1808? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
